# Direct dependencies for requirements.lock.
#
# Reconstructed 2026-09-27. This file was never committed, so the command named in
# requirements.lock's header could not be run. It is recovered from the 16 packages
# that requirements.lock itself marks with "# via -r requirements.in"; the remaining
# 23 pins are the transitive closure and must not be listed here.
#
# Regenerate with:
#     pip install pip-tools
#     pip-compile --output-file=requirements.lock requirements.in
#
# Constraints below mirror pyproject.toml ([project.dependencies] and
# [project.optional-dependencies].dev) rather than the pins, so a bump is made in one
# place and the other follows. These two files are two declarations of the same set:
# keep them in sync.
#
# Caveat for whoever regenerates: requirements.lock's header records that it was
# resolved with Python 3.14, while pyproject.toml declares requires-python >= 3.11.
# A resolution performed on 3.14 is not evidence for 3.11, and part of the current
# pin drift against a 3.11 machine comes from that alone. Decide the target
# interpreter first and pass it explicitly (--python-version), and note that
# httpx2 is not yet a declared dependency even though newer Starlette prefers it
# for TestClient.

# --- runtime (app/ may import these) ---
